Fix: XMA decoder output ended one frame short, hanging the loading screen

Applies to: rexglue-sdk nightly-20260921-923c1a59 (thirdparty/rexglue-sdk)
Apply with: git -C thirdparty/rexglue-sdk apply ../../patches/rexglue-sdk/0001-xma-release-held-back-frame.patch
Status:     local patch, not upstream yet (worth reporting)

WHAT BROKE
  Crash's first loading screen froze forever with no sound. Its audio load
  task decodes a 30,208-sample XMA sound effect into memory and busy-waits
  (sub_8234C580, a 50 us poll loop) until it has every sample. The SDK's
  XMA decoder only ever produced 29,824.

WHY
  Since commit f24299f ("XMA loop wraps silenced the loop's last 192
  samples"), XmaContext::Decode() emits every frame one decode late: it holds
  the decoded frame in carry_frame_ and completes it with the head of the
  next decoded block, to line FFmpeg's 192-sample-early output up with the
  bitstream. When the input runs dry, no next block ever arrives, so the
  last frame of every stream is never output. Real hardware has no such lag.

  A second, older bug surfaced once the frame was released: the
  "consume-only" path in Work() (no input left, just draining) never cleared
  output_buffer_valid when it filled the output ring exactly. write == read
  then looked *empty* to the game instead of *full*, and a whole buffer of
  samples (1,024 here) vanished.

THE FIX (src/audio/xma_context.cpp, include/rex/audio/xma/context.h)
  1. New XmaContext::FlushCarry(): when no input buffer is valid and nothing
     is pending, output the held-back frame (its 320 decoded samples + 192
     samples of silence standing in for a block that doesn't exist).
  2. Work() calls it before the consume-only path, and that path now waits
     for output room like the decode loop does (RingBuffer::Write doesn't
     clamp to free space).
  3. The consume-only path drops output_buffer_valid when the ring is full.

  Trade-off: if a game is merely *late* with its next input buffer (an
  underrun), 192 samples at that seam become silence. The sample count
  still matches hardware, which is what games synchronize on.

HOW WE FOUND IT: docs/findings/04-loading-hang-xma.md

